    "product_id": "70b1c37a-8fff-4483-a06a-66e48f675731",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161151\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n451 AM PDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms expected today\n  and Thursday across our eastern zones.\n\n* Elsewhere, expect breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts through \n  the week with dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpecting isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon across Lincoln and Mohave counties, with chances\nspreading into Thursday morning. While the southern Great Basin \nremains situated under a dry southwesterly flow aloft, the \nsouthern Mojave Desert will experience a south-southeasterly flow,\nwhich will usher in 1.25 to 1.75 inches of PWAT into the region -\nspecifically southeastern Nevada and northwestern Arizona. In \naddition to the moisture, there is a disturbance riding up the \nBaja California that is currently highlighted by the NHC as having\na 10 percent chance of tropical storm formation in the next 48 \nhours. While this disturbance is unlikely to become a cyclone, as \nit pushes northward, the vorticity maxima associated with it will \nlead the development of showers and thunderstorms across southern \nMohave County / the lower Colorado River Valley this afternoon. \nAdditionally, energy riding along the upper-level trough across \nthe western CONUS has closed off over northern California this \nmorning and will begin its meander eastward across Nevada this \nafternoon. The enhanced jet associated with this closed low will \ncombine with daytime heating to facilitate convection across \neastern Lincoln, eastern Clark, and Mohave counties, which is in-\nline with the latest CAM guidance. That said, there is a noticable\nPWAT gradient across the center of our forecast area, which could\nact as a dryline, resulting in convection developing a bit \nfurther west than guidance currently suggests. Will continue to \nkeep an eye on locations along and west of Interstate 15 as the \nday progresses. As a result of ample instability and moisture, \nthunderstorms that form are expected to be efficient rain- makers \nwith frequent lightning, which will elevate the risk of flash \nflooding, particularly across Mohave County and underneath \ntraining storms. Forecast HRRR soundings in south-central Mohave \nCounty show over 1750 J/kg of SBCAPE, 750-1000 J/kg of DCAPE, 1.50\ninches of PWAT, and 500 mb cooling (LIs around -4 degrees C), so \nsevere thunderstorms are likely as well.\n\nThe trough axis associated with the aforementioned closed low will \npush through the forecast area today and Thursday, resulting in \nelevated south-southwesterly winds with gust speeds generally \nbetween 25 and 35 mph. Cannot rule out occasional gusts to 40 mph \nin the higher terrain. Otherwise, persistent troughing over the \nwestern CONUS will result in afternoon high temperatures near-\nnormal for mid-September. Cloud cover heading into overnight \nperiods may result in elevated morning lows, however.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty\nsoutheasterly winds will develop by mid-morning, with gusts to \naround 20KT expected. Early afternoon, winds veer to the south and\nincrease, with gusts around 25KT expected and sporadic higher \ngusts closer to 30KT possible. While a brief lull in winds may be \npossible after sunset, there is higher confidence that gusty winds\nwill persist through late evening before diminishing overnight. \nThunderstorm activity is expected to remain east of the terminal \nover northwestern Arizona and the Mormon Mesa corridor, with VFR \nconditions prevailing. FEW-SCT clouds with bases around 10kft can \nbe expected this afternoon and tonight, with temperatures \nremaining under 100 degrees.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will become\ngusty out of the southeast by mid- morning, before shifting to \nthe south to south-southwest by early afternoon. Gusts to around \n25KT will be common across the region, with slightly stronger \ngusts to around 30KT possible in the northern Owens Valley and \nalong the Lower Colorado River Valley where up-valley winds will \ndevelop. Winds across the western Mojave Desert will generally \nremain westerly, with gusts to 20-30KT expected to develop mid- \nafternoon and continue into the night. Elsewhere, gusts will \ndiminish by mid- to late evening. Thunderstorms are expected this \nafternoon and evening over eastern areas, and especially over \nnorthwestern Arizona, with impacts from lightning and gusty \noutflow winds possible at both KEED and KIFP. FEW-SCT clouds with \nbases at or above 10kft are expected this afternoon onward for \nmost of the region, with lower ceilings and greater coverage in \nthe vicinity of thunderstorms.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
